    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p68-q62-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$x=\\dfrac{t+1}{2}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$t>-3$.",
          "worked": "The graph has $x>-1$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{t+1}{2}&>-1\\\\t+1&>-2\\\\t&>-3\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p68-q62-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$x=2^t-1$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$t\\in\\mathbb R$.",
          "worked": "For every real $t$, $2^t>0$, so $2^t-1>-1$. Conversely, every $x>-1$ gives $t=\\log_2(x+1)\\in\\mathbb R$. Thus $t\\in\\mathbb R$ gives exactly the displayed section.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p68-q62-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$y=t^2+4t+4$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$t>-3$ if $x=t+2$; $t<-1$ if $x=-t-2$.\n\nComplete the square, then choose either linear branch for $x$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}y&=t^2+4t+4\\\\y&=(t+2)^2\\end{align*}$$\nSince $y=x^2$, either $x=t+2$ or $x=-t-2$ is suitable.\nFor $x=t+2$:\n$$\\begin{align*}t+2&>-1\\\\t&>-3\\end{align*}$$\nFor $x=-t-2$:\n$$\\begin{align*}-t-2&>-1\\\\t&<-1\\end{align*}$$\nEach choice covers the displayed section with its corresponding restriction.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 108
      },
      {
        "id": "p68-q62-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$y=1-t$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No such parametrisation covers the whole displayed section.\n\nBoth points with $x=\\pm\\tfrac12$ have the same $y$ and hence the same $t$.",
          "worked": "Both $\\left(-\\tfrac12,\\tfrac14\\right)$ and $\\left(\\tfrac12,\\tfrac14\\right)$ belong to the displayed section. From $y=1-t$, each requires $t=\\tfrac34$. A single-valued expression $x=f(t)$ cannot give both x-coordinates at that same parameter value. Therefore no such parametrisation covers the whole displayed section.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 76
      }
    ]
  },
